<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
      The Spring Boot "starter parent" gives us two things for free:
        1. Dependency management - we can list dependencies WITHOUT a <version>,
           and Spring Boot picks versions that are known to work together.
        2. Sensible plugin defaults - the compiler, the test runner, and the
           spring-boot-maven-plugin are all pre-configured.
      Without it we would be hand-picking dozens of compatible version numbers.
    -->
    <parent>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
        <version>4.1.1</version>
        <relativePath/>
    </parent>

    <!-- groupId + artifactId + version = this project's unique coordinates. -->
    <groupId>com.persona</groupId>
    <artifactId>persona-monolith</artifactId>
    <version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <name>persona-monolith</name>
    <description>persona - signup/login/profile CRUD, built as a single deployable monolith</description>

    <properties>
        <java.version>21</java.version>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <!--
          spring-boot-starter-webmvc brings in: Spring MVC (@RestController, @GetMapping),
          Jackson (Java objects <-> JSON), and an embedded Tomcat server.
          This is why the app can serve HTTP without us installing a server.

          NAME CHANGE IN SPRING BOOT 4: this was called `spring-boot-starter-web`
          in 3.x. Nearly every tutorial and StackOverflow answer you will find still
          says `-web`. On Boot 4 that artifact does not resolve - if you ever see
          "Could not resolve dependencies ... spring-boot-starter-web", this is why.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webmvc</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          Day-03. JDBC, a connection pool (HikariCP), JdbcClient, and Spring's
          DataSource auto-configuration. NOT spring-boot-starter-data-jpa — there
          is no Hibernate here, no @Entity, no magic. We write the SQL.

          That is deliberate. Day-04 adds JPA, and it should arrive as a
          labour-saving device over something already understood, not as the
          only way you have ever seen a row reach an object.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-jdbc</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          The PostgreSQL driver: the code that actually speaks Postgres's wire
          protocol over a socket. <scope>runtime</scope> because nothing we write
          imports org.postgresql — we compile against java.sql interfaces, and
          the driver is only needed when the program runs.

          That scope is the three-layer rule expressed in the build file: if this
          were <scope>compile</scope>, nothing would STOP a class from importing
          the vendor directly.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
            <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          Flyway. Versioned, ordered, immutable SQL migrations that run
          automatically at startup.

          The alternative is a human typing CREATE TABLE into a terminal once,
          which means the schema exists only in production and in that person's
          memory. With Flyway the schema is CODE: in git, reviewed, and identical
          on every machine. It records each applied migration in a
          flyway_schema_history table, so running the app twice does not run the
          migration twice.

          flyway-database-postgresql is a separate artifact since Flyway 10 —
          the core no longer bundles per-database support.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
            <artifactId>flyway-core</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
            <artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <!--
          SPRING BOOT 4 CHANGE, and it cost real debugging time.

          In Boot 3, flyway-core on the classpath was enough: the auto-
          configuration lived in the single spring-boot-autoconfigure jar and
          switched itself on. Boot 4 split that jar into per-technology modules,
          so FlywayAutoConfiguration now ships in spring-boot-flyway.

          The failure mode is what makes this worth a comment. Nothing errored.
          Flyway was on the classpath, the migration was on the classpath, and
          the application started perfectly — it simply never ran the migration
          and never logged a word about it. The symptom appeared one layer away
          as  relation "users" does not exist.

          How it was found: the classpath was searched for the class itself
          (unzip -l over every spring-boot jar) rather than trusting that the
          dependency being present meant the feature was active. Worth
          remembering — "the jar is there" and "the auto-configuration exists"
          are different claims.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-flyway</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          Test-only. Bundles JUnit 5, AssertJ, Mockito and Spring's test support.
          <scope>test</scope> means it is on the classpath when running tests,
          but is NOT packaged into the deployed jar.

          Also renamed in Boot 4: was `spring-boot-starter-test` in 3.x.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webmvc-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!--
              Repackages the compiled classes + all dependencies into ONE
              executable "fat jar", so `java -jar target/persona-monolith.jar` just runs.
              It is also what makes `mvn spring-boot:run` work.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>

            <!--
              Surefire runs *Test by default and ignores *IT. That default is
              good — it keeps `mvn test` fast and free of infrastructure — but
              it means JdbcUserRepositoryIT would never run at all.

              So we add *IT to the includes, and the class itself carries
              @EnabledIfSystemProperty(named = "it", matches = "true"). The
              result:

                  mvn test              30 fast tests, no database needed
                  mvn test -Dit=true    those plus the real SQL against Postgres

              The guard lives in the test rather than in a Maven profile so that
              the reason it skipped is visible in the test report, instead of the
              class silently not existing.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <includes>
                        <include>**/*Test.java</include>
                        <include>**/*IT.java</include>
                    </includes>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
